pub const LESSON_TEXTS : [27]str = [
    "Vibecoding means you describe what you want in plain words and an AI agent writes the program. Your part does not end there. You run it, and you check the result against your description, because a program can finish without an error and still be wrong. In the spec of this lesson a cafe tab is the number of drinks times the price of one drink: 3 drinks at 4 each is 12. A run that adds instead of multiplying prints 7, finishes cleanly, and is rejected by the check. The check is a test, and the test judges the result, not the person or the program that wrote it. The key distinction is between running and being right. A program can do the wrong thing cleanly, with no error message. A reliable way to tell is to write down the expected answer before you run and compare it to what the program gave. When an agent writes the code for you, writing that expected answer is still your job: the agent does not know what you wanted, only what you described. Saying it precisely enough that a comparison can be made is the core habit of vibecoding. If the comparison ever fails, that is not a problem with the test  --  it is the test doing its job. The task asks you to change one number and watch one test turn red; that proves the test is real, not a check that passes on every input regardless of what the program did.",
    "A program is a list of small steps that the computer does one after another. Take the number 2. Add 3, then double it: you get 10. Double it, then add 3: you get 7. The same two steps in another order give another answer, so when you describe a program to an agent you describe the order too. A loop is one step written once and repeated: add 3 four times, from 2, gives 14. When a result surprises you, read the steps in order and find the earliest one that does not do what you meant. The order of steps is the program. When you say 'do A and B', you have left the order open and the agent may pick either. Say 'do A, then B' to fix it. A loop is not shorthand for a human reader  --  it is a precise instruction to the computer meaning 'do this exact step this many times'. When a result is wrong, start reading at the top, follow each step by hand, and stop when you find a step whose output does not match what you expected. That step is the one to change. Working backwards from the wrong output is slower and less reliable than reading forwards from the beginning. Describing a goal without describing the path leaves the agent to pick one, and not always the one you meant. A loop that runs the wrong number of times and a loop that runs the right number of times with the wrong step are two different problems with two different fixes; reading the steps in order tells you which one you have.",
    "Five sentences about vibecoding sound right and are not. If it runs without an error, it is right: the average of 4 and 8 written as a plus b over 2 prints 8, and the answer is 6. One passing test proves the code is right: x times x equals x plus x at 2, and not at 3. The agent remembers the whole conversation: it reads a window, 8 messages of the 20 sent in this case. A step can always be undone: 7 halved and doubled is 6. A check that passes is a good one: one that says yes to everything also says yes to 2 against 3. Each myth is a small case, and a test settles it. Each myth is seductive because it sounds like a reasonable rule. Myth one fails because a wrong formula can run cleanly: producing the wrong number is not an error. The second fails because a test only checks the specific inputs it was given; different inputs may still fail. The third fails because the agent's context window is a fixed size, not the whole conversation. The fourth fails because operations like integer halving lose information and cannot be reversed. The fifth fails because a check that accepts every answer proves nothing at all. The lesson's approach is to treat any claim as a myth until you find a counterexample that breaks it. A specific case with a specific number is stronger than any argument in words. That is what the spec does: each myth is paired with the exact input and output that breaks it.",
    "An editor is where you read and change files. An agent sits beside it: you describe a change in words and it edits the files for you. It edits fast, but you are the one who keeps or rejects each change. The rule in the spec is two checks: can you read the whole change, and do the tests still pass? A change of 3 lines that passes is kept. A change of 12 lines that fails is sent back. A rewrite of 90 lines is sent back even when it passes, because nobody has read it. The spec sets the reading limit at 20 lines; pick your own and keep to it. Reading a diff is not optional. When an agent makes a change, it shows you exactly which lines were removed and which were added. The job is to compare those lines to what you asked for. A diff can be correct by some definition but not what you wanted; reading is how you tell. The two checks exist because tests alone are not enough: a change can pass all the tests and still introduce something you did not ask for and would not want. Setting a limit on how many lines a diff may touch is the practical way to make reading feasible. Sending back a diff that is too large is not a failure  --  it is a request for a smaller step, which is easier to verify.",
    "The terminal is a window where you type a command and press Enter. Ten commands go a long way: pwd, ls, cd, mkdir, touch, cat, cp, mv, rm and clear. They are safe to try, with one exception: rm deletes a file with no undo, so read it twice before you press Enter. The spec lists all 10 with a one-line meaning each and marks exactly 1 of them as having no undo. pwd prints your current folder: run it first to know where you are before any command that depends on location. ls lists the files and folders in the current folder. cd moves into a named folder; cd followed by two dots moves one level up. mkdir makes a new empty folder. touch makes a new empty file. cat prints the full contents of a file to the terminal. cp copies a file to a new name or location. mv moves or renames a file. rm deletes a file permanently with no way to get it back. clear wipes the visible terminal so you can read from the top. The habit to build: run pwd before any command that depends on where you are, and read the full rm command carefully before pressing Enter. When an agent suggests a command, read it before running: the nine safe commands can be tried freely, but rm is the one exception.",
    "An error message looks like noise, but it has 4 parts in the same order every time: the kind of error, what it says, the file and the line. The line is where the problem was noticed, so the cause can sit just above it: start reading 1 line before. Keep 4 shortcuts at hand: Cmd+S saves, Cmd+Z undoes, Ctrl+C stops a program in the terminal, and the up arrow brings back the previous command. When you ask your agent for help, paste the whole message, not a retelling of it. The kind of error tells you which layer is reporting the problem. A syntax error means the code could not be parsed at all. A runtime error means the code ran but hit a situation it could not handle. A type error means the code tried to use a value in a way the language does not allow. The message tells you what specifically went wrong. The file and line tell you where the check failed; the cause is often just above, which is why the spec says to start reading one line before the reported line. When you ask an agent for help, copy the whole error block, not a summary: the agent needs the kind, the message, the file and the line together. A paraphrase strips out the parts the agent needs most. Common mistake: saying 'it shows something about undefined' instead of pasting the full message. The full message is always shorter to handle than the conversation needed to recover it.",
    "Vibecoding means an agent changes your files while you watch, and some changes break things. A commit is a save point: it keeps every file exactly as it was at that moment, and you can edit as much as you like afterwards, the saved copy does not change. In commit.t27 the file said 10 when it was committed; an agent then made it 99; going back returns 10, not 99. Each commit adds one save point to the history and overwrites nothing: after a third commit there are 3. Commit when the program runs, before you ask for the next change. A commit is an instruction to the version control system to take a snapshot of every file right now. The snapshot is permanent and future edits do not change it. If the program breaks after a commit, you can go back to the snapshot and the files will be exactly as they were. The message you write with each commit is a note to your future self: it names what state the project was in at that moment. The practical habit is to commit every time the program runs and the tests pass. That way each save point in your history is a working state, and going back always lands you somewhere that works. Between commits, any change the agent makes can be undone by going back; after a commit, the change is part of the history and the next commit starts from there.",
    "A branch is a copy of your line of work where you can try something, and the main line stays as it was until you decide. In branch.t27 main holds 10 and an agent's try turns it into 99 on its own branch. If the try is bad you delete the branch and main still holds 10. If it is good you merge it and main holds 99. While you try there are 2 lines of work, main and the try; after either ending there is 1. Ask an agent for a risky change on a branch, not on main. The main line is the stable version of your project. A branch is a parallel copy: changes on the branch do not appear on main until you merge them. This means you can ask for a risky change  --  a rewrite, a restructure, a large edit  --  and if the result is wrong you delete the branch without touching main. The step to follow: make sure your work is committed on main before creating a branch; ask for the change on the branch; test it; merge if it passes, delete if it fails. The 2 lines of work are independent while the branch is open. An edit on 1 does not appear on the other, and each can be read and tested on its own. Branching costs nothing except a name; the habit of branching for risky changes costs nothing and saves the whole project when a change goes wrong.",
    "To prove who you are to a code host without a password you make a key pair: a public half you hand out and a private half that never leaves your machine. The host keeps the public half and sends a challenge that only the private half can answer. In key_pair.t27 a pair has 2 halves, 1 is shared and 1 is kept, and 0 copies of the private half are ever sent to a host, a chat or a repository. The key text in the spec is a made-up placeholder, not a key. Never paste a real private half into a prompt: an agent that sees it has it. The public half is safe to put anywhere: in the host settings, in a config file, in a profile. The private half proves you are who you say you are, and it must never leave your machine. The host does not need the private half to verify you: it sends a challenge  --  a piece of data it chose  --  and your machine answers using the private key. The host checks the answer against the public key it already has. Because the private key was never sent anywhere, it cannot be stolen in transit. The practical rule: put the public key in the host once; the private key stays in a local file, never in a prompt, a repository or a chat message. If you ever paste a private key somewhere by mistake, treat it as lost and generate a new pair.",
    "A prompt that works has four parts: a goal, the context the agent cannot see, the limits it must keep, and the check that tells you it is done. In prompt_parts.t27 each part is one bit of a number, so a prompt becomes a mask: the vague prompt Make it better is mask 0, a goal alone is mask 1, and a full prompt with all four parts is mask 15. The tests read the bits straight from the constants. The lesson plants one bug: give the check the same bit as the limit, and the four parts no longer add up to 15, so a test fails. The goal tells the agent what you want. The context gives it information it cannot see on its own: the name of the file, the shape of the data, what the program already does. The limits tell it what not to do: do not change the existing tests, do not add new files. The check says how you will know the work is done: the function returns a specific value for a specific input, the page loads without an error. A prompt without a check is open-ended, and the agent will decide when it is done. A prompt with a check gives both of you the same definition of done. The mask model in the spec makes this testable: each part contributes one bit, so a complete prompt has all four bits set and the mask equals the maximum value.",
    "An adjective such as bigger or shorter fits many rules, so the agent picks one of them, and not always yours. An example input with the output you want fits far fewer. In prompt_example.t27 the rule you mean is add 3, and the examples are 1 to 4, 2 to 5 and 5 to 8. The opening example fits two rules, add 3 and multiply by 3 then add 1, so it cannot choose between them. The second example, 2 to 5, rules the other one out, because it would give 7. The lesson plants one bug: change the step from 3 to 2, and the examples fail. An adjective fits many programs; an example input with its expected output fits far fewer. Two well-chosen examples can narrow a rule down to one. The technique: before asking an agent for a function, write down three inputs and the outputs you expect. Use examples that are most different from each other, because similar inputs often agree even on a wrong rule. The agent reads your examples as constraints and writes code that satisfies them. If the examples are precise enough, code that satisfies them is exactly the code you wanted. The spec uses this as a testable claim: changing one example breaks the constraint and forces a test to fail.",
    "A big request is a chain of small ones, and the agent can get any link wrong. In small_step.t27 a plan has five steps, and each ends with a check you run before the next prompt. The list PASSED holds 1 for a step whose check passed and 0 for one that did not: 1, 1, 0, 0, 0. The earliest zero is step 3, so step 3 is the single prompt to fix. One big prompt for all five steps leaves five suspects; one small step leaves one. The lesson plants one bug: say that step 4 was the earliest to fail, and the test that reads the list catches it. Breaking a request into small steps is not about being cautious  --  it is about knowing where to look when something goes wrong. A big prompt that produces a bug anywhere leaves every part of it as a suspect. A small prompt, one that changes one thing and runs one check, narrows the suspect to that one thing. The practical habit is to end every prompt with a check: a command to run, a test to pass, a specific output to look for. Only after the check passes do you move to the next prompt. When a check fails, you have exactly one prompt to look at, not five. The spec makes this precise: each step in the plan has a pass or fail recorded, and the earliest fail is the step to revisit.",
    "Every piece of work with an agent has the same four phases. Idea: you say in one sentence what you want. Build: the agent writes one small piece. Test: you run it and a test says yes or no. Ship: you save it and put it where it is used. The phases are not a straight line. When the test says no, the work goes back to build, not forward to ship, and the loop runs again. The spec of this lesson holds two tables: where each phase goes next when the tests pass, and where it goes when they fail. The player runs its 4 tests. Change one entry of the fail table so that a failed test goes on to ship, and exactly one test goes red. The cycle is idea, build, test, ship. When the test passes, the work moves forward. When the test fails, the work goes back to build: the agent tries again. Ship only happens after a test passes. This is what separates vibecoding from sending one big request and hoping: each small build is validated before the next one starts, and nothing reaches ship without passing a test. The 2 tables in the spec are the full definition of the loop: 1 maps each phase to its next phase when things go right, and the other maps it when things go wrong.",
    "A round has 4 steps. Ask the agent for one change. Run the program. Read what came back, all of it. Fix one problem and go round again. Each round removes one problem, so a program with 3 problems is green after 3 rounds. The spec keeps a table of the problems left after each round: 3, 2, 1, 0. The shortcut that breaks the loop is a round that says done without reading the result. In the widget you can switch the reading off and watch the counter lie. In the spec the same shortcut is one changed number, and exactly one test goes red. The 4 steps in order: ask, run, read, fix. Each step depends on the one before. You cannot fix a problem you have not read, and you cannot read a result you have not run. Skipping the read step breaks the whole loop: the program appears to improve because you are not checking, and problems accumulate instead of being removed. The discipline is to read the full output of every run, even when it is long, before deciding what to fix next. A run with no visible output is still a run to read: it means nothing went wrong, and that is the result. The spec names the shortcut that lies: the counter shows zero problems because nobody is reading, not because they are gone.",
    "Getting stuck is normal; going round in a circle is the signal to change what you do. Count how many times in a row the same error came back. The opening time, read the error and say in your own words what it claims. The second time, ask for a smaller step. The third time, go back to the last save point, the commit that worked. After that, start a fresh conversation with a short summary and the one error. A different error resets the count, because it means you got past the last one. The spec holds this as a table of moves indexed by the count, and its 4 tests check each rung. The count is the key number: how many times in a row the same error appeared. Seeing it once is a normal problem. The second time is a signal to change approach: smaller step, different wording. The third time means the approach is stuck, and going back to a known-good commit is the right move. Beyond that, a fresh conversation breaks any loop the current one has locked itself into. What you bring to the fresh conversation is short: a summary of what you are building and the one error you are seeing. Everything else is in the files, which the agent can read. A different error always resets the count: seeing a new error means you have made progress past the previous one.",
    "You ask an agent for a function that gives the larger of two numbers. Do not read its code yet: first write down the answer you expect for 3 and 9, which is 9. A test runs the code and the judge compares: yes if it gave 9, no if it gave anything else. The judge sees only the answer, so a yes means the code agrees with you for those inputs and nothing more. A second version of the function with a slip in it, always giving the leading number, gives 3, and the judge says no. The lesson behind the judge: a test is a claim that the function gives the right answer for specific inputs. It says nothing about inputs it has not checked. A yes from a test means the code and the test agree on those inputs; it does not mean the code is correct everywhere. To trust a test, you must have seen it fail: a test that has never been red may be checking the wrong thing, or not checking at all. The practice of planting a bug  --  deliberately breaking the code to see the test go red  --  is how you confirm the test is real. If the test stays green with the bug in, the test is not checking what you thought. Write the expected answer before you read the code; this keeps the test honest and independent of the implementation.",
    "A test that has never failed may be looking in the wrong place. Take the rule that an adult is 18 or older and plant a bug: change the sign so that 18 itself no longer counts. A weak test on ages 30 and 5 stays green with the bug in, because neither age is near the edge. A test on age 18 turns red. Bugs live at edges, so put a test there, and plant a bug to see it fail. An edge is a value where the rule changes. For the adult rule, the edge is 18: below it the answer is no, at it and above the answer is yes. A weak test that only checks values well inside each zone will not find a bug that moves the edge. A strong test checks the value at the edge, the value just below it, and the value just above it. The technique of planting a bug is how you check that your test is real: change the code so it is definitely wrong, then run the test. If the test stays green, it is not checking the right thing. If the test turns red, you have confirmed it is watching the edge you care about. Bugs found by edge tests are often the ones that cause problems in real use, because real inputs tend to cluster near limits and boundaries. After planting the bug and seeing the test go red, put the original code back and confirm the test goes green again: that is the full proof that the test is correctly positioned.",
    "An agent says done and shows a diff: the lines it removed and the lines it added. Read it against what you asked for. If you asked for a change to lines 10 to 20 and the diff also touches line 40, that is a change nobody requested, and now you have to read it. Accept a diff whose every touched line is in the request. Send back one that is not, or ask why. Even a clean diff still goes through the tests. A diff is the agent's answer to your request, written as a list of changes. Reading the diff is the check between asking and accepting. The diff can be right in the sense that the tests pass and still be wrong in the sense that it changed something you did not ask about. Changes you did not ask for and did not read are changes you now own but cannot explain. The rule is to accept a diff only when every line it touches was in your request. When a line outside the request appears in the diff, send it back and ask for a version that stays within scope. This keeps the project legible: at any point in the history you can read back the request for a commit and understand every line that commit changed. The tests are still run after accepting a diff; a diff inside scope can still introduce a failure, so reading and testing are both required, not either-or.",
    "When you chat with an agent you read free text. When a program reads the answer, free text is a problem: it cannot tell a sentence from a request to run something. So the agent is asked for a reply with a shape: a kind, and the arguments that kind needs. This spec has 3 kinds: 0 say, 1 ask and 2 call a tool. A sentence needs no argument; a question and a tool call need at least 1. The program checks the shape first and acts only if it fits. A tool call that names no file is refused before anything runs. The negative control plants the bug in one place: set NEEDS_CALL to 0 and the test the_bad_reply_is_refused turns red, because a reply with no arguments is no longer too short. Free text is easy for a human to read but hard for a program to interpret consistently. A structured reply has an agreed shape: a type code that says what kind of reply it is, followed by the arguments that type needs. The program reads the type first and decides what to do based on that; it only reads the arguments once it knows the type. This separation means the program can refuse a reply whose shape does not fit before it tries to act on it. The shape check is a gate: a tool call with no arguments never reaches the tool, because the shape check stops it first. The lesson teaches this by encoding the reply shape as a small number and showing what changes when 1 part of the shape is missing.",
    "An agent only reads and writes text. It cannot open a file or run a test by itself. A tool is a function the host runs for it: the agent names the tool, the host checks the name against a list, runs it and sends the result back as text. This spec lists 3 tools: 0 read a file, 1 run the tests and 2 write a file. Reading and testing change nothing, so they run at once; writing changes your project, so it waits for your yes. The loop is capped too: of 9 calls asked in one turn the host lets 5 through, so a stuck agent cannot call tools forever. Plant the bug by setting FREE_BELOW to 3 and the test writing_waits_for_a_yes turns red: writing would run without asking. A tool is a named function the host knows how to run. The agent requests it by name and arguments; the host runs it and sends the result back as a message. From the agent's side, all actions look like text: it names a tool, it gets text back, it replies with more text. The distinction between read and write is real: reading a file and running tests do not change anything, so they can be run immediately; writing a file changes the project, so the host asks for your confirmation first. The cap on tool calls per turn is a safety rail: an agent that has looped into a problem cannot call tools in an infinite loop, because the host stops letting calls through after the limit is reached.",
    "An agent does not remember your project; it sees the last messages of the chat, as many as fit in its window. The spec uses a window of 6 and a chat of 10, small enough to trace by hand. The agent keeps 6 messages and forgets 4: messages 0 to 3 have fallen out, and the oldest one it still sees is number 4. What you write into a file of the project stays, because the agent can read the file again in a new chat. That is why a rule you want kept goes into a file, not into one more message. Plant the bug by setting WINDOW to 7 and two tests turn red, because the window no longer ends where the chat does. The context window is the agent's working memory: it sees everything in the window and nothing outside it. As the conversation grows, the oldest messages fall out of the window and the agent loses access to them. This is why a rule said early in a conversation may be forgotten later: when the message that stated it has fallen out of the window, the rule is gone from the agent's view. The fix is to put rules and decisions into files: a file stays in the project and the agent can read it at the start of a new conversation. Chat messages are ephemeral; file contents are permanent. The practical habit: when you decide something that should hold for the whole project, write it into a file immediately.",
    "A bot is not magic. It is a loop with four beats: wait for a message, read it, pick a reply, send it, and wait again. In message_loop.t27 a message is a small number: 1 is start, 2 is help, 3 is stop, and anything else is not understood. A table maps each message to a reply, so one turn of the loop is one lookup. The inbox holds 5 messages and the loop stops at the stop message, so it answers 4. Send the widget a few messages and notice that the same message always gets the same reply. The key property of this loop is that it is deterministic: the same message always produces the same reply, every time. There is no memory between turns; each reply depends only on the current message, not on what came before. The loop runs as long as there are messages; a stop message ends it. The table that maps messages to replies is the entire brain of this bot. To change what the bot does, you change the table. To add a new message type, you add a row. The lesson uses small numbers as messages to keep the table readable, but the same structure works for text messages: map the message to a category, then the category to a reply. The inbox in the spec is a fixed list; a real bot's inbox is a stream that arrives over time, but the loop structure is the same: wait, read, pick, send, repeat.",
    "You do not write a bot, you describe it. spec_to_bot.t27 lists 3 commands and the reply each one gets, as data. Before anything is generated, 3 checks run on that description: every command has a reply, no two commands share a reply, and the number of routes equals the number of commands. A description that fails a check would make a wrong bot, so the table is fixed first and the bot is generated afterwards. Blank a reply in the widget and see which check turns red. The order matters: check the description first, generate the code second. A description that passes all 3 checks is correct by definition: every command maps to exactly 1 reply, no 2 commands lead to the same place, and the total number of routes is exactly the number of commands. A bot generated from a correct description is correct too. The checks run before generation so that a bad description never reaches the generator. This is the core pattern: data with checks, then code from data. The spec is the description, the checks are the proof of correctness, and the generated code is a consequence of both. When you change the description, the checks run again before the bot is regenerated: the checks guard every version of the bot, including later ones.",
    "A secret is a value that opens something, such as the token of a bot. The code names it, BOT_TOKEN, and never holds it. The value lives in a file called .env on your machine, and git is told to ignore that file. The repository keeps only .env.example, with a made-up placeholder. In secrets_out.t27 a file that holds a secret is never a file git saves, and the same goes for a message to an agent: write the name of the secret, not its value. A secret that was ever saved or pasted must be treated as lost and replaced. The rule is: the name lives in the code and the repository; the value lives only on the machine that needs it. Code that uses a secret refers to it by name and reads the value at startup from the environment or a local file. The repository holds only a template file that shows the shape of the secret  --  the name and a placeholder, never the real value. When you work with an agent, the same rule applies: tell the agent the name of the secret and ask it to read from the environment; never paste the value into the prompt. A secret that has been seen by any system outside your machine must be treated as known to that system. Generating a new secret and revoking the old one is the fix.",
    "An agent can build only what you can say. The smallest thing you can say about an idea has three parts: one input, one output and one test that tells you it is done. Take a made-up reading log: the input is one book title, the output is how many books are logged, and the test is that after one title the count is 1. The spec of this lesson holds those three as numbers and checks them with 4 tests. Ready means one of each: 1 x 1 x 1 = 1. Set the test count to 0 and the tests that name it go red, because an idea with no test is not ready to hand over. Your own idea can be as small as this one. The 3 parts together define the smallest handable idea. Without an input, the agent does not know what it is working with. Without an output, it does not know what to produce. Without a test, neither of you knows when it is done. An idea that has all 3 parts can be handed to an agent as a precise instruction. An idea that is missing any part requires a conversation to fill in what is missing, and a typical opening is 'what should the input be?' or 'how will you know it worked?'. Answering those questions before you send the prompt is the preparation this lesson teaches.",
    "When your project runs, pick one thing to measure and read it from the run itself. Made-up example: the reading log was run on 4 titles and kept 3. The share kept is 3 x 100 / 4 = 75 out of 100. That is the whole fact: one run, one number. It does not say what the next run will give, so the widget lists that as nothing yet. The spec has 3 tests: a measure needs at least one run, a run cannot keep more than it was given, and the share is read from the run. Set KEPT to 5 and two of them go red. A measure is a number read from a real run. It is not an estimate, a guess or a goal; it is the value the program printed when it ran on actual input. 1 run gives 1 measure; a second run may give a different one. Neither run predicts future runs: a measure answers only 'what happened this time'. The practice is to decide what to measure before the run: pick 1 thing the program can print, run it, and read the number. That number is a fact. Only after several runs can you say how the number changes  --  and even then only for the runs you have seen. The spec's constraint that a run cannot keep more items than it was given is a sanity check: a share above 100 out of 100 means the measurement is wrong.",
    "A plan for the next month is honest when each line is one of two kinds: a thing you measured, with the run that printed it, or a thing you do not know yet, with the run that would tell you. A line that promises a result nobody has measured does not belong in it. The made-up plan for the reading log has 5 lines: 2 measured, 3 unknown and 0 promised, and 2 + 3 + 0 = 5. The spec has 4 tests: the lines add up, something is measured, something is unknown and nothing is promised. Set PROMISED to 1 and two of them go red. This lesson gives no forecast of any kind: it is a way of writing, not a prediction. The 2 allowed kinds of line are very different. A measured line says: I ran the program, it printed this number, and that is what I know. An unknown line says: I have not run the experiment yet, but here is the run that would tell me. A promised line says neither: it names a future result that has not been measured and no run is specified to measure it. Promised lines are excluded because they cannot be checked. A plan built only from measured facts and named experiments is auditable: anyone can run the experiments and either confirm the unknowns or add new measured lines. This lesson does not predict what your project will do; it teaches the form a plan must take to be honest."
];
pub const LESSON_TASKS : [27]str = [
    "Open the spec in the player and run it: 3 tests pass. Then change RAN_TAB from 12 to 7 and run it again: the test the_run_matches_the_wish turns red. Put the 12 back.",
    "Find the three programs in the spec: add_then_double, double_then_add and add_three_n_times. Change LOOP_RESULT from 14 to 11 and run it: a_loop_is_one_step_repeated fails, because 11 is three steps, not four. Put the 14 back.",
    "Run the spec: 6 tests pass. Change AVG_RAN from 8 to 6 and run it: myth_1_runs_but_wrong fails, because the run no longer matches what the wrong program prints. Then write a sixth myth of your own as a case with numbers and a test.",
    "In the spec, find READ_LIMIT and the three proposed changes. Change the last KEEP to 1 and run the player: the test that names a change too long to read fails. Then put it back to 0.",
    "In the spec, find the list NO_UNDO. Mark cat with a 1 as well and run the player: the test that counts the commands with no undo fails. Then put it back to 0.",
    "In the spec, find ERROR_LINE and START_LINE. Change START_LINE to 15 and run the player: the test that wants the start one line above the error fails. Then put it back to 13.",
    "In the widget, read the value of the file at the three moments and find the one where the agent's edit is undone; then in the spec frame find RESTORED and say what would change if it were set to EDITED.",
    "In the widget, compare the two endings in the table and say which one changes main; then in the spec frame plant the bug MAIN_AFTER_DISCARD = 99 and see which tests turn red.",
    "In the widget, find the half that may be pasted into a host and the half that goes nowhere; then in the spec frame plant PRIVATE_COPIES_SENT = 1 and read which test turns red.",
    "Open the widget and find the one prompt that is ready to run; then in the spec change CHECK from 8 to 4 and run the tests to see which one fails.",
    "Open the table and find the row where rule B stops matching; then in the spec change A_STEP to 2 and count the tests that fail.",
    "In the diagram find the earliest step whose check failed; then in the spec change FIRST_FAILED from 3 to 4 and run the tests.",
    "Open the widget and walk an idea to ship with one failed test on the way; count the steps. Then in the spec find the entry of the fail table that sends a failed test back to build, change it to 3 and read which test goes red.",
    "Run the three rounds in the widget, then switch the reading off and run one round. In the spec change the 1 in the table to 0 and name the test that goes red.",
    "In the widget press the same error three times and name the move it picks. Then press a different error and see the count start again. In the spec change the move for a count of 3 from 2 to 1 and read which test goes red.",
    "Write down your answer for 3 and 9, run both versions, and read what the judge says to each. Then try 9 and 3 and see which version still gets a yes.",
    "Flip the sign, run the weak tests and the boundary test, and see which one turns red. Then put the sign back and see all three go green. In the spec, find the test that asserts age 18 is lost to the planted rule.",
    "Read the two diffs against the request for lines 10 to 20, mark the lines outside it, then accept one diff and send the other back. In the spec, find the line number the sprawling diff touches outside the request.",
    "Open the widget and find which kind needs no argument; then run the spec, set NEEDS_CALL to 0, and read which test fails and why. Put the line back and run it again.",
    "Open the widget and name the two tools that run at once; then run the spec, set FREE_BELOW to 3, and read which test fails. Put the line back and run it again.",
    "Open the widget and find how many messages a chat of 10 forgets in a window of 6; then run the spec, set WINDOW to 7, and read which two tests fail. Put the line back and run it again.",
    "Send start, then help, then an unknown word, then stop, and count the replies. In the spec frame, find the inbox and the index of its stop message, and the test that turns red when two commands share a reply.",
    "Blank one reply and name the check that turns red. In the spec frame, find the test that a missing reply breaks, and the one line you would change to plant that bug.",
    "Switch the real settings file to saved and read the alarm. In the spec frame, find the test that fails when TRACKED says the real settings file is saved, and the line you would change to plant it.",
    "In the spec, set IDEA_TESTS to 0 and read which 2 tests fail; then put it back. Then write your own idea as one input, one output and one test, one sentence each.",
    "In the spec, set KEPT to 5 and read the 2 tests that fail; then put it back. Pick one thing in your own project that a run can print, and write down how you will read it.",
    "In the spec, set PROMISED to 1 and read the 2 tests that fail; then put it back. Write 5 lines for your own project and mark each one measured or unknown; if a line is neither, cross it out."
];

; --- Claims --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
test the_course_is_three_cubed {
    assert LESSONS_PER_MODULE == 3;
    assert MODULE_COUNT == 9;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 27;
    assert MODULE_COUNT * LESSONS_PER_MODULE == LESSON_COUNT;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 3 * 3 * 3;
}

test it_starts_with_the_idea_and_ends_with_an_own_project {
    assert MODULE_IDS[0] == "idea";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[0] == "idea";
    assert LESSON_IDS[0] == "what-vibecoding-is";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[0] == "vc-describe-run-check";
    assert MODULE_IDS[8] == "own";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[26] == "own";
    assert LESSON_IDS[26] == "an-honest-plan";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[26] == "vc-honest-plan";
}

test the_desk_and_the_save_points_come_before_the_craft {
    assert MODULE_IDS[1] == "desk";
    assert MODULE_IDS[2] == "saves";
    assert MODULE_IDS[3] == "words";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[3] == "vc-editor-agent";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[6] == "vc-commit";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[9] == "vc-prompt-parts";
}

test the_judge_comes_before_the_agent_and_the_bot {
    assert MODULE_IDS[4] == "loop";
    assert MODULE_IDS[5] == "judge";
    assert MODULE_IDS[6] == "agent";
    assert MODULE_IDS[7] == "bot";
    assert LESSON_IDS[15] == "a-test-is-the-judge";
    assert LESSON_IDS[16] == "plant-a-bug";
    assert LESSON_IDS[18] == "the-mind-of-an-agent";
    assert LESSON_IDS[21] == "a-bot-is-a-message-loop";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[23] == "vc-secrets-out";
}

test the_course_sends_nothing {
    assert SENDS_NOTHING == true;
    assert LOCALES[0] == "en";
    assert LOCALES[1] == "ru";
}
